---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('firepush')!;
const path = '/apps/firepush';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/firepush');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/firepush');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Firepush currently send email and SMS campaigns?",
    "answer": "Its November 2026 notices say it is now a web push app and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. Older indexed articles may still describe those channels. Use the current notice and account-specific support information when planning continuity."
  },
  {
    "question": "Has Firepush shut down?",
    "answer": "The current website advertises web push plans, automations and Shopify installation. A change in channel scope is not evidence that the company has shut down."
  },
  {
    "question": "What are the current published push prices?",
    "answer": "The pricing page lists 500 monthly pushes free, Basic at $13 per month for 10,000 pushes and Pro at $49 for 25,000. Higher volume tiers are available. Check the selected tier and account billing before committing."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens at the push limit?",
    "answer": "Firepush says it warns at 80% usage and pauses push campaigns at 100% until reset or a tier change. Unused pushes do not carry over. Include peak release volume in your continuity plan."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does web push reach every mobile shopper?",
    "answer": "No. Reach depends on permission, browser and device requirements. Firepush says iPhone shoppers must add the store to the Home Screen. Verify support and activation for your store rather than treating all mobile sessions as subscribers."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can an old SMS list become a push audience?",
    "answer": "Do not assume phone numbers or email addresses confer browser push permission or transferable push subscriptions. Ask about supported exports and migration mechanics; collect the appropriate permission for each destination channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Firepush offer more than cart reminders?",
    "answer": "Its current pages advertise welcome, price-drop, back-in-stock, order, shipping and winback push as well as promotional campaigns. Confirm the trigger and eligibility behavior for the specific automation you intend to use."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should push coordinate with email and SMS?",
    "answer": "Choose a named owner for each audience and message. Test purchase suppression and exclusions across the actual tools. Do not assume a tag or shared email address creates automatic cross-provider synchronization."
  },
  {
    "question": "How can a store judge incremental value?",
    "answer": "Compare a push-eligible treatment group with a comparable holdout where practical, account for purchases credited to other channels and subtract operating costs. Click-attributed revenue alone is not proof of incremental recovery."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Web push and channel continuity</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Firepush currently offers Shopify web push campaigns and automations. Its November 2026 notices say SMS and email campaigns are no longer offered. Evaluate push reach, send limits and coordination with your existing email or SMS provider, and treat older three-channel instructions as historical.</p>
    <p class="mt-5 leading-8">Evidence checked November 25, 2026. This review uses supplier documentation and proposed evaluation scenarios; no merchant account tests or campaign outcomes are claimed.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Current notices take precedence over old three-channel copy</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/sms-campaigns/">SMS article</a> and <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/targeted-email-marketing/">email article</a>, updated November 18, 2026, explicitly state that Firepush now provides web push and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. This is affirmative supplier evidence of scope, not an inference from an unavailable page.</p>
      <p>Older indexed help, SMS-credit references and legacy account records can coexist with that change. Preserve the information needed to resolve an old balance or export request, but do not use it to promise a new email or SMS campaign. The current website continues to offer web push; the channel change does not establish company closure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">What remains useful about push</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/">current Firepush website</a> advertises cart, welcome, price-drop, stock and order-related push. Browser permission gives a store another way to contact subscribed shoppers. Product-specific alerts can match an expressed interest without requiring a long newsletter creative process. These are advertised capabilities, not our observed campaign outcomes.</p>
      <p>Whether push adds value depends on your reachable subscribers and purchase context. A gadget shopper returning to the same browser may be a useful recovery candidate. A streetwear subscriber requesting a release alert has a different purpose. Neither catalog type proves a recovery rate, delivery latency or sales advantage over email.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Legacy email and SMS continuity inventory</h2>
      <p>For an existing merchant, start with an inventory of campaigns, automations, subscriber records, consent evidence, opt-outs, sender identity, templates, balances and reporting history. Mark each item as still accessible, exported, awaiting support or rebuilt elsewhere. A historical article is not confirmation that an old workflow continues sending.</p>
      <p>Ask support about account-specific access, exports, credits and channel transition terms. Save the response with an owner and due date. Do not promise a refund, automatic migration, number portability or a universal historical cutoff without written evidence. Prioritize customer-facing gaps such as missing order messages separately from promotional campaigns.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Permissions and migration are channel-specific</h2>
      <p>Keep email opt-outs and SMS suppression history with the corresponding records. A shopper who allowed browser notifications has not thereby requested texts; an old phone list does not become a valid push audience. Confirm which subscription data can be exported and which subscriptions a destination can actually use.</p>
      <p>Before retiring a tool, retain necessary suppression and reporting records through an agreed process. Verify destination suppression after import, including a deliberately opted-out test contact. For push, ask about browser subscription portability and supported domains. Do not assume changing an app preserves every token or that an email address identifies every browser.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Desktop cart rehearsal: a $130 gadget</h2>
      <p>Proposed exercise, not a merchant result: allow push on a supported browser, abandon a $130 gadget cart and inspect the reminder link, variant and price. Then repeat with a purchase before the queued reminder. Firepush advertises reminders that stop after an order; the rehearsal checks that behavior in your setup.</p>
      <p>Repeat with a cleared cart, unavailable item and a different browser. Log permission state, event time, expected send and observed trace. An email-only visitor is not a failed push delivery if they never subscribed to push. Count eligible subscriptions before calculating conversion and distinguish delivery from a clicked notification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Stock and price alerts: relevance after the trigger</h2>
      <p>Proposed restock exercise: a shopper waits for a specific size while a different size returns. Check the intended eligibility and link before releasing a broad alert. If stock sells out while notifications are queued, confirm what the shopper sees on arrival. Avoid promising inventory reservation or sub-minute delivery unless separately established.</p>
      <p>For a $240 lamp discounted by 15%, the new price is $204 and the reduction is $36. Check the notification against the landing page, sale expiry and any shipping exclusion. This tests price accuracy; it does not imply that 12% of recipients will buy. A useful product alert can still fail if its destination is stale.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Streetwear release choreography without invented benchmarks</h2>
      <p>For a hypothetical limited sneaker release, list audiences by actual push, email and SMS permission, including people in more than one group. Define which message serves early access, public launch and follow-up. One release owner can reconcile the calendar across providers without assigning a permanent channel hierarchy.</p>
      <p>Rehearse a VIP purchaser just before the public announcement, a sold-out variant and an opted-out browser. Set frequency and expiry around the release rather than copying an arbitrary two-per-week or 72-hour rule. Check that a delayed alert does not advertise an expired allocation. No opt-in percentage, click threshold or sell-through promise is established by this scenario.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Device reach must be checked on the actual store</h2>
      <p>Firepush’s homepage describes modern desktop and Android support and an iPhone Home Screen requirement. Follow its <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/help/web-push-notifications/">current web push help</a> for device setup and ask support about store-specific availability. General browser support is not proof that every visitor can subscribe in the same way.</p>
      <p>Use a device matrix: permission prompt available, subscription completed, notification received, destination opened and unsubscribe respected. Record the browser and operating system version for each attempt. This makes an audience estimate defensible without assuming all Safari visitors are unreachable or all mobile visitors can receive push.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Budget sends and limit continuity</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/pricing/">pricing page</a>, checked November 25, 2026, prices push volume rather than three-channel impressions. Budget automated sends as well as launches. An illustrative 200 subscribers receiving two messages consumes 400 pushes; one more message to all 200 takes the plan demand to 600, beyond the 500 free allowance.</p>
      <p>Assign someone to review remaining allowance before a launch and decide whether to raise the tier, narrow the audience or reschedule a promotion. Confirm how pauses affect the specific automation you rely on. A free plan can be useful for evaluating behavior; it is not a promise of unlimited transactional continuity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Contribution and incrementality with transparent assumptions</h2>
      <p>Illustrative economics only: suppose a push-eligible experiment produces 8 genuinely incremental orders at $25 contribution each. That is $200 before platform and operating cost. Subtract a $13 Basic plan and 2 hours of staff work at $30 for $127 remaining. At 2 incremental orders, the same assumptions yield a $23 loss. These are neither observed Firepush results nor a prediction.</p>
      <p>Randomize eligible subscribers where practical and keep other channel treatment comparable. Count returns and offers consistently. Do not add push-attributed and email-attributed sales as if they were distinct orders. For scarce stock, a faster purchase may move an order earlier without increasing total sales; evaluate the objective you actually have.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Cutover, rollback and the next operator</h2>
      <p>Pause equivalent legacy promotional sends before enabling replacements and inspect pending queues. Test destination capture forms, opt-outs and order notifications before removing an old installation. Keep a rollback record naming the old and new owners, verification evidence and the authority to pause. No fixed three-week parallel run is needed to justify duplicate messages.</p>
      <p>Hand over a channel inventory, device matrix, alert eligibility rules, budget limits, support answers and a release calendar. Review exceptions after a campaign: stale links, sold-out items, quota pauses and duplicate contacts. Choose whether to retain push from demonstrated value and manageable work, without an automatic order-volume upgrade to another supplier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Related reviews and guides</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3">
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend review</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/recart">Recart review</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript review</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Cart recovery guide</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Firepush Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
